          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there a link between sleep and migraine?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nYes, the sources describe a link between sleep and migraine, and it runs in both directions. The lifestyle summary \"Sleep and migraine: regularity, and the relationship running both ways\" says that poor sleep can make attacks more likely, attacks can disturb sleep, and good sleep is one of the more reliable ways an attack ends.\n\nThe same summary says that a change in sleep pattern is one of the triggers people most often report. It also says regularity seems to matter more than the exact number of hours. Both too little sleep and sleeping in can set off an attack. The example it gives is a late Friday night followed by a long Saturday lie-in, which can bring on a weekend headache.\n\nThe sources do not explain how sleep and migraine are connected in the body, and they do not say how much sleep is \"right\" for any one person. If sleep problems or migraines are affecting your life, a doctor or other clinician can look at your pattern and your options with you.\n\nSources: lifestyle-migraine-sleep; condition-migraine (for the general description of migraine).\n\nIf you have a sudden, severe, or unusual headache, or a headache with weakness, confusion, speech changes, or vision loss, seek emergency care right away. The red-flag guidance (red-flag-sudden-severe-headache) covers these patterns.",
